# TripPilot AI: Master Prompt for Google AI Studio (Build)

HOW TO USE
1. Open Google AI Studio > Build. Paste everything from "BEGIN MASTER PROMPT" to "END MASTER PROMPT" as ONE message.
2. If the output is cut off or has errors, send the staged prompts in Section Z one at a time ("Continue with Stage 2", and so on).
3. In AI Studio, add the secrets in Settings: GEMINI_API_KEY (required). Optional: GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY, GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID, Firebase config.

---

## BEGIN MASTER PROMPT

You are a senior full-stack engineer, motion designer and UX designer. Build a complete, responsive, production-quality web app called **"TripPilot AI"**: an intelligent travel planning, booking, live-safety and replanning app. It feels like MakeMyTrip (flights, trains, buses, hotels, packages), but a Gemini-powered AI travel agent plans trips, monitors them live, detects risks and automatically replans when disruptions occur.

Deliver COMPLETE, runnable, TypeScript-correct code: every file in full, no placeholders, no "TODO", no "rest of code here". The app must compile and run with the demo working end to end without any paid travel API.

### 0. TECH STACK AND RULES
- React 18 + TypeScript + Vite, Tailwind CSS, Framer Motion, React Three Fiber (`@react-three/fiber`), `@react-three/drei`, `three`, Lucide React, Zustand (with `persist` middleware), `@google/genai` for Gemini, Leaflet + `react-leaflet` (OpenStreetMap fallback map), `canvas-confetti`, `react-markdown` (+ `remark-gfm`) for AI replies, `vite-plugin-pwa` for the offline PWA.
- **Secrets:** never hardcode keys. Read from environment (`process.env.GEMINI_API_KEY` as injected by AI Studio, or `import.meta.env.VITE_*`): Gemini key, Google Maps key, Google OAuth client ID, Firebase config. Provide `.env.example`.
- **Gemini usage:** model `gemini-2.5-flash`. For every call use `responseMimeType: "application/json"` with a `responseSchema`, validate the parsed result (Zod or a manual type guard), wrap in try/catch with an 8s timeout and one retry, show a skeleton loader while waiting, and on any failure fall back to realistic hardcoded demo data and show a small **"Demo data"** badge (assistant chat shows an **"Offline mode"** badge).
- Use realistic Indian rupee pricing, real Indian cities, railway stations, airports and realistic travel times (for example, Chennai Central > Mettupalayam by Nilgiri Express, then road to Ooty). Mock datasets: flights, trains, buses, hotels, packages, weather, traffic, hospitals, police stations, accident-prone zones, expenses.
- Code quality: functional components, strict TypeScript types, `React.lazy` + `Suspense` for pages and all 3D scenes, `React.memo`/`useMemo`/`useCallback` where useful, an ErrorBoundary around every 3D canvas and every page, no `any` unless unavoidable.
- All 3D models are built ONLY from simple low-poly geometry (box, cylinder, cone, icosahedron, octahedron, sphere with low segments, extrude of simple shapes) with `meshStandardMaterial` / `meshBasicMaterial` and `flatShading`. NO external GLTF/model/texture files. Cap `dpr={[1,2]}`.

